 salinity and oligocor  concentration all need to be optimized  but that will only get you so far uh  a lot of chemical modifications are  actually introduced into these molecules  so shown on the right is a short is a  residue from a short living nucleotide  therapeutic  and modifications are done uh at the  backbone so these include phosphorous  thyroid linkages the sugar region  two primo methyls and two prime fluoros  are often added at the two prime  position to render the reactive hydroxyl  group uh inert  uh you don't see these as often but you  can also have base modifications  including 5-methylcytodene  and in some instances entirely different  chemistries are used such as  phosphodamide morpholinos  and peptide nucleic acids  so really all these chemical  modifications are added to kind of  increase the stability  of the oligonucleotide so that it won't  be  degraded once it enters the body  the second  property that needs to be improved are  the drug delivery  won't i won't be discussing this very  much in this talk but  common techniques involve using lip and  nanoparticles or an acetylgalactosamine  conjugation  so there are plenty of different areas  uh during the drug manufacturing and  distribution process where  impurities can be generated  common techniques cur current analytical  techniques to  characterize the purity  and identity of  short oligonucleotides include mass  spectrometry and liquid chromatography  but these uh oligonucleotides can  undergo a variety of different  degradation pathways including deep  urination which is shown on the right  in this  case a adenine or guanine base is  removed and that leaves an a basic site  these are considered  harmful  due to the great measures the body goes  through to actually resolve these a  basic sites  other examples include hydrolytic  cleavage so the oligonucleotide is  broken up into smaller units uh  desulfurization so the phosphorous  thyroids  lose their sulform and get replaced with  oxygen you can also have different  cross-linking reactions  and deamination  so all of these  different degradation pathways can lead  to potentially harmful  impurities  and if they're not harmful they at least  would lower the drug efficacy  therefore techniques are needed to  not only  identify the impurities but also  determine the level of impurity  solution nmr is a high-resolution  structural technique that  provides  information on short-lived nucleotides  under full formulation conditions and  these methods could nicely complement  current mass spec and  liquid chromatography based approaches  so shown on the left in a is a sirna  and if we highlight all of the hydrogens  shown in b you can see that these are  very well dispersed throughout the  entire molecule  and all of these hydrogens are  readily  accessible via nmr techniques  so for each hydrogen atom so there's in  panel b a purple box around one of the  hydrogens  you get a particular signal in an nmr  spectra shown in c  so the chemical shift is along the x and  this provides information on the local  chemical environment and hence structure  surrounding that  hydrogen and the intensity of that  signal conveys information on  dynamics  so if you collect these types of  measurements throughout the entire  molecule you get an overall picture  of the structure and dynamic  of the  oligonucleotide  so as our group has demonstrated with  monoclonal antibodies nmr data can be  used as inputs  for chemometric analysis to determine  subtle changes in structure and purity  the goal of my project is to extend  these methods from monoclonal antibodies  into short oligonucleotide therapeutics  to aid in the development of our nmr  methods  we developed a model s i rna  shown here  for nmr benchmarking purposes  the model sign rna is modeled after  currently fda approved drugs and  includes a lot of the chemical  modifications  that are found  class including two primo methyls two  prime fluoros  and phosphothyroid linkages  we also have a randomly generated  sequence to remove any sequence bias  and it's capped with terminal gc base  pairs for stability  this sr model s iron is also prepared at  natural isotopic abundance to match the  conditions in which these drugs are  synthesized and it's formulated in a  buffer  closely matching those of other  fda approved therapeutics  within the model sir a you have both  what we call native and non-native  chemistry so the native chemistries uh  include  your bases like  adenosine guanosine  uridine and cytobean which are found in  all rnas or  in some dnas but then we also have  non-native chemistries and these include  uh bases like 5-fluorouridine  as well as some of the modified sugars  like 2-prime fluoro and 2-primal methyl  and you also have non-native chemistries  at the backbone regions such as  phospholiphthylates  so initially i'll discuss 1d nmr  fingerprints so these methods report on  a particular type of atom so shown here  we have  all the hydrogens so those are proton  nmr  and b you have all the phosphorus  and then in c you have all the fluorines  and as you can see these nuclei are  dispersed throughout the molecule in  varying degrees  the hydrogens are found throughout  whereas the phosphoruses are found along  the backbone  and then the fluorines are kind of  sprinkled in throughout the molecule  at the sites of either 5-4 u or 2-prime  floral modification  so the first  data i'll show you is from the  1d proton nmr and as you can see  these spectra are rather complex  this is due to the over 400 hydrogens  that are actually located within the sir  and a  which is shown in a so each one of those  hydrogens potentially gives rise to a  signal  and  there is only a limited amount of space  in chemical shift over which all of  those signals can resonate so in this  case it's about 15 ppm  so we can kind of break up the different  regions into belonging to a particular  class for instance around  1 to 2 ppm we have the methyl h7s  within you know the the one to  nine ppm range it's very hard to assign  these  or uh determine much about them from  just a simple 1d proton experiment the  exception being the amino region which  is boxed and uh  kind of expanded  around the 12 to 14 ppm range  these signals report on hydrogen bonding  so in the event of a base pair the amino  proton is protected from solvent  exchange and therefore gives rise to a  detectable signal whereas in the absence  of base pairing these signals broaden  beyond detection  so essentially each peak represents a  single hydrogen bond and one thing  you'll notice is we have numbers above  all of these signals  and these represent assignments so in  contrast to our previous work with mobs  which are these very large very complex  proteins  the relative simplicity  simplicity of the sir and a therapeutics  allows for their chemical shift  assignment which adds  a new level of detail so basically  atomic level insight  into the srna  which is very nice  another 1d experiment we can do is a  phosphorus measurement so  an a is the sirna with all of its  phosphorus is shown as spheres  and as you can see we have two kind of  clusters that show up in the 1d  phosphorus experiment  we have a group of signals around 0ppm  these correspond to the phosphates so  phosphorus bound to oxygen  and around 60 ppm we have the  phosphorous thyroids  so we can see that there's a very large  separation in chemical shift between the  phosphothioids and the phosphates  however within each group a lot of the  signals are kind of piled on top of each  other making it very hard to  learn much about them  with the phosphor thyroids so remember  that each one of these phosphorthiolates  can be in one of two diastereomers  either the rp or the sp configuration  and as it turns out the different  diastereomers resonate in different  windows  which is shown in b so even though this  region is rather cluttered  we can actually  uh  pick out the different diastereomers and  this kind of 1d spectra serves as  kind of a fingerprint for the overall  distribution of the different  diastereomers  within the oligonucleotide sample and  can therefore help in ensuring batch to  batch reproducibility  the last 1d measurement i'll show you is  for the  fluorine  spectrum is the fluorine spectra so  these are probably the most uh  simplified spectrum  due to the fewer number of fluorines  than phosphorus and hydrogen  and as you can see here both the five  fluorous and the two prime floors they  resonate in distinct chemical shift  regions and we're able to assign these  so even though there are fewer fluorines  compared to the hydrogens and the  phosphoruses  due to their distribution throughout the  oligonucleotide  they actually capture a lot of the  structure of the olympia  so in conclusion the 1d nmr fingerprints  provide a rapid initial samp assessment  of the sample quality but are relatively  low resolution  these experiments can be acquired with  in about 30 seconds as is the case with  the 1d proton measurements  the phosphorus and the fluorine  measurements can be completed in about  five minutes so really they're rather  rapid  kind of methods  one way we can improve the resolution  and their is by extending  the 1d fingerprint into a second  dimension so shown here  in a is the sirna in b we have all the  hydrogens  shown  and what we can do within a 2d nmr  measurement is now select only the  hydrogens that are attached to a  particular heteroatom  which in this case is going to be  nitrogen  and as you can see that therefore cuts  down the number the total number of  hydrogens that we're looking at now  we're only looking at hydrogens that are  bound to nitrogen  for those of you that are unfamiliar  with what a 2d nmr data set looks like  this  little schematic should help out  so here in a we have the sirna with all  the proton nitrogen pairs labeled  each one of those proton nitrogens  is going to give a signal  that's going to look like something in b  so  just like with the 1d measurement we  have the proton chemical shift along the  x  but now along y we have the heteroatom  chemical shift so in this case  n15  and then kind of in the third dimension  coming out of the page  in the z direction this is your  intensity  so just like a 1d measurement a 2d  correlation has information on  the local chemical structure via the  chemical shifts of the two  atoms the proton and the nitrogen but it  all and it also has information on  dynamics  and that's  the  intensity  in the  sir rnas there are several uh  both native and non-native chemistry  that can act as nmr probes  so you have the proton nitrogen  correlations these include  the aminos so these are involved in base  pairing  you can also have a long-range aromatic  proton nitrogen experiment  and then you also have proton carbon and  these come in a variety of different  versions so you have your aromatics  methyls two primal methyls and  deoxyribose and you can select the  different correlations by tuning the  scalar coupling between  the hydrogen and its  and the heteroatom  we also have  proton fluorine correlations that are  possible with these modified oligos  as well as proton phosphorus  correlations which i will not really  discuss in this talk  so here is a 2d amino proton nitrogen  experiment and as you can see  it's similar to the 1d amino spectrum  this defines the overall secondary  structure of the  nucleotide  what's nice about the 2d version though  is now you have your  you or  5 floor u signals separated from the g  signals by the nitrogen chemical shift  so you'll see at the top around 145 to  150 ppm all the g amino signals resonate  and then around 160 to 165 ppm in the  nitrogen dimension this is where all the  use  and fluorous resonate  so this is a very nice experiment and it  once again each signal corresponds to a  particular  base pair  one of the limitations of this method  though  is that you need to be at relatively  cool temperatures and  slightly acidic buffers in order to  ensure that the exchange rate of those  immunoprotons is low and therefore you  get decent signals  one way to bypass that is to do a  so-called long-range proton nitrogen  experiment in which case the adenine h2s  are correlated to the nitrogen n1 and  m3s  and the resulting spectra is shown here  for each a we have  each a in the sequence you get  signals for each of those and what's  nice about this once again is that you  don't need  cool temperatures and you don't need  acidic buffer conditions to get really  good data  one of the main limitations with the  proton nitrogen experiments are the  relatively long experiment times which  range from half a day for the amino  region  to one and a half days for this long  range proton nitrogen experiment  as shown here is a 2d aromatic proton  carbon spectra and this provides  information on base stacking  so this  the proton carbon correlations report on  both base paired and unbase paired  regions  and they are unaffected by solvent  exchange unlike the amino proton  nitrogen correlations  as you can see for our model s i rna we  have three distinct regions defined by  the carbon uh chemical shift  so up at the top of the spectrum you  have all the five floor uracils  uh in the middle you have the adenine  guanine  cytodine uridine and thymodine  aromatic  proton carbon correlations and at the  bottom you have all the  adenine h2c2 correlations  what we can also do with proton carbon  experiments is we can look at some of  the  non-native chemistry so shown here is  the  two primal methyl  spectra  normally in nucleic acids in particular  rnas the sugar regions are relatively  inaccessible by nmr due to the close  proximity of these signals to the water  resonance  but with these modifications you're  actually able to  shift a lot of these resonances far  enough away from the water that  uh the the signals are relatively  unprepared by the water suppression and  shown here as an example these are the  protons carbons from the two primal  methyl region  we can also do a  fluorine detected  fluorine proton measurement  and  once again uh  this is due to the non-native  chemistries of the short oligonucleotide  therapeutics that we can actually use  this technique and what's really nice  about fluorine is that it is virtually  background free and that none of the  excipients or  buffers contain fluorine so the only  signals that you'll see here  are actually from the  model sir a  and so these are very nice correlations  to have  all right so in conclusion the 2d nmr  methods they offer improvements in  resolution and selectivity  but they come at the cost of a slightly  longer experiment time so in the case of  the proton nitrogen correlations  these experiments tend to take around  half a day to one and a half days  the proton carbon correlations can be  acquired in as little as an hour for the  two primal methyl region  and as long as four hours for the  aromatic region  and then the hf correlations these can  take  probably around  four to eight hours and the same thing  for the proton phosphorus correlations  okay so all the data i've shown so far  has been on our model si rna what do  these data actually look like on a  fda-approved  drug  so to answer that we have a simulated  sir a drug product  give us siren which is shown here  zero siren contains a lot of the same  chemical modifications that were  represented in the model si rna  including two prime  o methyls and two prime fluoros and  phosphorous thyroid linkages  uh an additional uh  feature of gibbous iron is that it  contains this l96  down mac ligand that's conjugated at the  three prime end of the send strand and  this is to aid in drug delivery  so this  l96 ligand  is is unique to give osiris  in comparison to the model sir rna  shown here are the 1d nmr fingerprints  of give oscar and as you can see they  report on a variety of structural  features both at the base in the case of  the amino proton region the sugar as  shown here with the two prime fluoros in  the backbone  i'm showing both the phosphate and the  phosphorous thyroids  the 2d nmr data set  that i've i'm showing here these are the  amino correlations and as you can see  everything is nicely  resolved you have to use  resonating around 160 to 165 ppm in the  nitrogen chemical shift  and all the g's resonate around 145 to  150 ppm in the nitrogen chemical shift  and here we have a proton carbon uh  fingerprint this is actually of the  galmac ligand  so this is unique to  gilos siren in comparison to the model  si rna  so we're actually able to get a  fingerprint of that  galmac ligand  so in conclusion  i hope i've convinced everyone that nmr  spectra can serve as structural  fingerprints of oligonucleotide  therapeutics  one of the  great things about this class of  molecules are that their relative  simplicity  allows for their chemical shift  assignment  which is something that's lacking in  a lot of the monoclonal antibody work  just due to their large size and  complexity  these nmr methods can be tailored to  report on both native and non-native  chemistries  and based on  their distribution throughout the whole  oligonucleotide they provide  high resolution information on structure  and dynamics  the structure of the nucleotides is  encoded in their peak positions and  intensities all of which can be  leveraged through chemometric analysis  to detect subtle perturbations and  structures  and stability  and this work we hope will  be able to  aid in the drug development and  distribution process  here are a brief list of references  should anyone want to read more  and with that i'd like to thank the  event organizers as well as my  two mentors here at ibbr dr  robert brunson and dr john marino  i'd also like to thank some of our  collaborators so dr jace jones and anne  tran at  university of maryland uh baltimore for  their uh  complementary mass spec  work on this project and also dr  christina braganzo  who's done a lot of  computational work to  help out with this project  and with that i'd be happy to answer any  questions you may have for me thank you

