hod  though  is that you need to be at relatively  cool temperatures and  slightly acidic buffers in order to  ensure that the exchange rate of those  immunoprotons is low and therefore you  get decent signals  one way to bypass that is to do a  so-called long-range proton nitrogen  experiment in which case the adenine h2s  are correlated to the nitrogen n1 and  m3s  and the resulting spectra is shown here  for each a we have  each a in the sequence you get  signals for each of those and what's  nice about this once again is that you  don't need  cool temperatures and you don't need  acidic buffer conditions to get really  good data  one of the main limitations with the  proton nitrogen experiments are the  relatively long experiment times which  range from half a day for the amino  region  to one and a half days for this long  range proton nitrogen experiment  as shown here is a 2d aromatic proton  carbon spectra and this provides  information on base stacking  so this  the proton carbon correlations report on  both base paired and unbase paired  regions  and they are unaffected by solvent  exchange unlike the amino proton  nitrogen correlations  as you can see for our model s i rna we  have three distinct regions defined by  the carbon uh chemical shift  so up at the top of the spectrum you  have all the five floor uracils  uh in the middle you have the adenine  guanine  cytodine uridine and thymodine  aromatic  proton carbon correlations and at the  bottom you have all the  adenine h2c2 correlations  what we can also do with proton carbon  experiments is we can look at some of  the  non-native chemistry so shown here is  the  two primal methyl  spectra  normally in nucleic acids in particular  rnas the sugar regions are relatively  inaccessible by nmr due to the close  proximity of these signals to the water  resonance  but with these modifications you're  actually able to  shift a lot of these resonances far  enough away from the water that  uh the the signals are relatively  unprepared by the water suppression and  shown here as an example these are the  protons carbons from the two primal  methyl region  we can also do a  fluorine detected  fluorine proton measurement  and  once again uh  this is due to the non-native  chemistries of the short oligonucleotide  therapeutics that we can actually use  this technique and what's really nice  about fluorine is that it is virtually  background free and that none of the  excipients or  buffers contain fluorine so the only  signals that you'll see here  are actually from the  model sir a  and so these are very nice correlations  to have  all right so in conclusion the 2d nmr  methods they offer improvements in  resolution and selectivity  but they come at the cost of a slightly  longer experiment time so in the case of  the proton nitrogen correlations  these experiments tend to take around  half a day to one and a half days  the proton carbon correlations can be  acquired in as little as an hour for the  two primal methyl region  and as long as four hours for the  aromatic region  and then the hf correlations these can  take  probably around  four to eight hours and the same thing  for the proton phosphorus correlations  okay so all the data i've shown so far  has been on our model si rna what do  these data actually look like on a  fda-approved  drug  so to answer that we have a simulated  sir a drug product  give us siren which is shown here  zero siren contains a lot of the same  chemical modifications that were  represented in the model si rna  including two prime  o methyls and two prime fluoros and  phosphorous thyroid linkages  uh an additional uh  feature of gibbous iron is that it  contains this l96  down mac ligand that's conjugated at the  three prime end of the send strand and  this is to aid in drug delivery  so this  l96 ligand  is is unique to give osiris  in comparison to the model sir rna  shown here are the 1d nmr fingerprints  of give oscar and as you can see they  report on a variety of structural  features both at the base in the case of  the amino proton region the sugar as  shown here with the two prime fluoros in  the backbone  i'm showing both the phosphate and the  phosphorous thyroids  the 2d nmr data set  that i've i'm showing here these are the  amino correlations and as you can see  everything is nicely  resolved you have to use  resonating around 160 to 165 ppm in the  nitrogen chemical shift  and all the g's resonate around 145 to  150 ppm in the nitrogen chemical shift  and here we have a proton carbon uh  fingerprint this is actually of the  galmac ligand  so this is unique to  gilos siren in comparison to the model  si rna  so we're actually able to get a  fingerprint of that  galmac ligand  so in conclusion  i hope i've convinced everyone that nmr  spectra can serve as structural  fingerprints of oligonucleotide  therapeutics  one of the  great things about this class of  molecules are that their relative  simplicity  allows for their chemical shift  assignment  which is something that's lacking in  a lot of the monoclonal antibody work  just due to their large size and  complexity  these nmr methods can be tailored to  report on both native and non-native  chemistries  and based on  their distribution throughout the whole  oligonucleotide they provide  high resolution information on structure  and dynamics  the structure of the nucleotides is  encoded in their peak positions and  intensities all of which can be  leveraged through chemometric analysis  to detect subtle perturbations and  structures  and stability  and this work we hope will  be able to  aid in the drug development and  distribution process  here are a brief list of references  should anyone want to read more  and with that i'd like to thank the  event organizers as well as my  two mentors here at ibbr dr  robert brunson and dr john marino  i'd also like to thank some of our  collaborators so dr jace jones and anne  tran at  university of maryland uh baltimore for  their uh  complementary mass spec  work on this project and also dr  christina braganzo  who's done a lot of  computational work to  help out with this project  and with that i'd be happy to answer any  questions you may have for me thank you
 

